WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.038 --> 00:00:10.249
FAYE MOSS: Welcome back to Superstars in History —the talk show featuring heavy-hitting interviews with people from

2
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:18.032
the past. I'm Faye Moss. Today's guest was a soldier, baseball player,

3
00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:26.242
and activist. He broke the color barrier when he became the first African American player in Major League

4
00:00:26.243 --> 00:00:47.977
Baseball. Straight from history, here is number 42 for the Brooklyn Dodgers, Jackie Robinson!

5
00:00:47.978 --> 00:00:50.806
JACKIE ROBINSON: It's great to be

6
00:00:50.807 --> 00:00:57.742
here, Faye. FAYE MOSS: Before you take us out to the ball game, let's start with

7
00:00:57.743 --> 00:01:05.492
your early life. You were born in Cairo, Georgia, in 1919. What was your

8
00:01:05.493 --> 00:01:10.807
childhood like? JACKIE ROBINSON: I was the youngest of five kids, and my father left our family when

9
00:01:10.808 --> 00:01:16.976
I was a baby. We then moved to Pasadena, California, where we were the only black family

10
00:01:16.977 --> 00:01:21.631
in the neighborhood. Once a girl called me racist names, so I called her

11
00:01:21.632 --> 00:01:27.506
names too. When her father threw rocks at me, I threw

12
00:01:27.507 --> 00:01:33.703
them back! FAYE MOSS: That must have been hard. Did being good at sports

13
00:01:33.704 --> 00:01:37.612
make things easier? JACKIE ROBINSON: It did help, because the other kids always wanted

14
00:01:37.613 --> 00:01:48.361
me on their team. In 1939 I went to the University of California, Los Angeles and played four sports: football, basketball, track and

15
00:01:48.362 --> 00:01:52.846
field, and baseball. Baseball wasn't my best sport; I was only a little

16
00:01:52.847 --> 00:01:57.344
interested in it. Back then I was more interested in a pretty student

17
00:01:57.345 --> 00:02:05.065
named Rachel! FAYE MOSS: You left college before graduating to help your mother, and you played semiprofessional football for

18
00:02:05.066 --> 00:02:08.325
a short time. But then World War

19
00:02:08.326 --> 00:02:14.003
II broke out. JACKIE ROBINSON: I was drafted into the army and spent two years there, but

20
00:02:14.004 --> 00:02:18.513
I never saw combat. I got into some trouble when I refused to move to the back of

21
00:02:18.514 --> 00:02:23.189
an army bus. I knew there were regulations against segregation

22
00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:26.923
on military vehicles. I was accused of insubordination but

23
00:02:26.924 --> 00:02:32.944
found innocent. After I was honorably discharged from the army, I worked as a college

24
00:02:32.945 --> 00:02:38.864
basketball coach. FAYE MOSS: How did you go from coaching college basketball to

25
00:02:38.865 --> 00:02:45.354
playing professional baseball? JACKIE ROBINSON: An army friend who played for the Kansas City Monarchs in the Negro Leagues told me

26
00:02:45.355 --> 00:02:49.874
they paid good money. Those leagues were formed so that black players could compete in

27
00:02:49.875 --> 00:02:57.496
their own league. I had never dreamed of playing baseball professionally, but there I was . . . accepting an offer from

28
00:02:57.497 --> 00:03:03.588
the team. I enjoyed the pay, but I disliked being away from Rachel, whom I was

29
00:03:03.589 --> 00:03:10.853
still dating. I also didn't like traveling constantly, because black players faced segregation all over the

30
00:03:10.854 --> 00:03:18.076
country. FAYE MOSS: That was the mid-1940s with Jim Crow segregation laws in large parts of

31
00:03:18.077 --> 00:03:25.027
the country. JACKIE ROBINSON: That's right. And some sports, like boxing, were already integrated.

32
00:03:25.028 --> 00:03:31.133
But not baseball. The general manager for the Brooklyn Dodgers, Branch Rickey, wanted to break

33
00:03:31.134 --> 00:03:35.552
baseball's color barrier. He was a religious man who thought segregation was

34
00:03:35.553 --> 00:03:40.557
morally wrong. He sent scouts into the Negro Leagues to look for the right man for his

35
00:03:40.558 --> 00:03:44.372
team. I met him in 1945 when I was

36
00:03:44.373 --> 00:03:47.394
26. FAYE MOSS: What made you

37
00:03:47.395 --> 00:03:56.982
the right player? JACKIE ROBINSON: To be honest, I was a risk. Mr. Rickey needed a strong athlete who could withstand racist taunts, abuse,

38
00:03:56.983 --> 00:04:03.090
and even death threats. All my life I'd fought back against injustice, but now I'd have

39
00:04:03.091 --> 00:04:08.562
to hold back. Mr. Rickey tested me by yelling terrible things at me to make sure I could

40
00:04:08.563 --> 00:04:17.472
handle it. I realized I couldn't lose my temper, because I'd ruin not just my own chance but also an opportunity for every black

41
00:04:17.473 --> 00:04:23.463
athlete. After I joined the team, Mr. Rickey became like the father that I never had.

42
00:04:23.464 --> 00:04:33.805
Rachel and I got married in 1946, and I spent a year in the minors playing in Canada for the Montreal Royals—one of Brooklyn's affiliate teams. FAYE

43
00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:39.329
MOSS: April 15, 1947 was a truly historic day.

44
00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:47.601
You were called up to play for the Brooklyn Dodgers—the first black player in the Major Leagues!

45
00:04:47.602 --> 00:04:55.232
JACKIE ROBINSON: Yes, but before that season started, some of my teammates had talked about signing a petition to avoid playing with

46
00:04:55.233 --> 00:05:00.375
me. Across the National League, players threatened to strike, but nothing came of it.

47
00:05:00.376 --> 00:05:07.696
I felt uneasy on Opening Day, standing alone at first base, the only black man on the field. FAYE

48
00:05:07.697 --> 00:05:10.462
MOSS: Was your first season

49
00:05:10.463 --> 00:05:16.141
difficult? JACKIE ROBINSON: It was. One reporter called me the loneliest man

50
00:05:16.142 --> 00:05:20.622
in baseball. I faced some trouble early on when we played the

51
00:05:20.623 --> 00:05:25.195
Philadelphia Phillies. Their manager called me terrible racist

52
00:05:25.196 --> 00:05:29.017
names. I'd had similar experiences in the minors

53
00:05:29.018 --> 00:05:34.302
too. Pitchers threw at my head, runners kicked me and spiked me with their cleats.

54
00:05:34.303 --> 00:05:40.242
Once a fan threw a black cat on the field, saying, "Hey, Jackie, here's your cousin!"

55
00:05:40.243 --> 00:05:46.137
Rachel supported me through everything. I couldn't have done it without her. FAYE

56
00:05:46.138 --> 00:05:50.378
MOSS: On the bright side, you were named Rookie of the Year.

57
00:05:50.379 --> 00:05:55.906
And the next year, there were more black players in the MLB.

58
00:05:55.907 --> 00:05:58.776
JACKIE ROBINSON: And that took some pressure off of

59
00:05:58.777 --> 00:06:07.047
me! We had a great season. The Dodgers were league champions but lost the World Series to the

60
00:06:07.048 --> 00:06:15.481
Yankees. My best year was 1949. I played in the All-Star Game and was named Most Valuable Player when the season ended. In

61
00:06:15.482 --> 00:06:20.710
1955 we finally beat the Yankees to win the World Series! I

62
00:06:20.711 --> 00:06:26.843
stole home in the first game, setting my record for home steals at 19. FAYE MOSS:

63
00:06:26.844 --> 00:06:30.582
You went on to play ten seasons for the Dodgers.

64
00:06:30.583 --> 00:06:33.234
What were your career highlights? JACKIE

65
00:06:33.235 --> 00:06:38.113
ROBINSON: When 1956 ended, the Dodgers announced they were going to trade

66
00:06:38.114 --> 00:06:40.897
me. I decided to retire

67
00:06:40.898 --> 00:06:50.699
instead. I ended my career with 972 runs scored, 1,563 hits, and 200 bases stolen.

68
00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:55.616
In 1962 I was inducted into the Baseball Hall of Fame. FAYE

69
00:06:55.617 --> 00:07:04.378
MOSS: Those are impressive statistics. And you went on to do some impressive activism work

70
00:07:04.379 --> 00:07:11.438
too. You were involved in the Civil Rights Movement and often marched beside Martin Luther King, Jr.

71
00:07:11.439 --> 00:07:16.913
JACKIE ROBINSON: The Civil Rights Movement was important to me, but I also got involved in other

72
00:07:16.914 --> 00:07:21.740
causes. My son, Jackie Jr., fought in Vietnam and returned home a drug addict.

73
00:07:21.741 --> 00:07:28.633
He overcame his addiction and went on to help others, but he died in a car accident at age 24. His

74
00:07:28.634 --> 00:07:32.419
experience led me to campaign against drugs. I

75
00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:38.294
realized the most important thing I could do was to impact others' lives. FAYE MOSS:

76
00:07:38.295 --> 00:07:40.947
I think you succeeded.

77
00:07:40.948 --> 00:07:47.035
Thank you for joining us, Jackie! We'll let you get back to history now.

78
00:07:47.036 --> 00:07:51.135
JACKIE ROBINSON: Thank you,

79
00:07:51.136 --> 00:08:01.413
Faye. FAYE MOSS: Nine days after throwing out the first pitch in the 1972 World Series, Jackie Robinson died of a heart attack at

80
00:08:01.414 --> 00:08:08.263
age 53. His jersey number, 42, was permanently retired

81
00:08:08.264 --> 00:08:17.765
in 1997. Jackie Robinson paved the way for people of color in Major League Baseball and is admired for his courage and bravery in facing

82
00:08:17.766 --> 00:08:29.697
adversity. In fact each year on April 15, every player, coach, and manager in the MLB wears number 42 to honor Jackie Robinson.

83
00:08:29.698 --> 00:08:36.931
That's all for now on Superstars in History . I'm Faye Moss.

